<!--Post: 3206--><!--Time: 1104617410-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3206"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Mike_kelleher<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mike_kelleher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 01, 2005 - 2:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->FMS and memory-filling are not the same but they are related. FMS has clinical DSM links and specific criteria by which it can be identified. Memory-filling is far more generic, pervasive, and not considered abnormal. In fact, it is a necessary and functional aspect of memory processing for all of us. For example, it&#39;s well known that eye-witness testimony is very unreliable yet it is rare for witnesses to suffer from FMS, and nearly as rare for them to suffer from another issue, such as PTSD.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3206-->
<!--Post: 3222--><!--Time: 1104636970-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3222"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 01, 2005 - 7:36 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Scbenson2001 <BR>I&#39;m not sure I&#39;d agree that 1974 was the height of the Cold War and to suggest having them hide in a closet was to help prevent harm from a nuclear attack is a bit thin but perhaps in Wichita... a tornado drill? That isn&#39;t what Charlie Otero said though. There is a thread here for speaking to Charlie that I&#39;m assuming he reads if not this one... perhaps we should ask. I don&#39;t think it would be insensitive in light of the fact that he is here... participating may be something he is or isn&#39;t willing to do...? <BR> <BR>Charlie or Tom any feed back on this idea?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3222-->
<!--Post: 3229--><!--Time: 1104643011-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3229"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 01, 2005 - 9:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Mike_Kelleher:  I hadn&#39;t thought through my suggestion well.  You&#39;re correct.  But what about changing your assumption on the accuracy of what C.O. said.  Does that affect your profile? <BR> <BR>Do we have to know absolutely whether Charlie Otero is accurate before we can talk about how it changes this case if he is? <BR> <BR> <BR>I re-thought the Otero killing assuming C.O.&#39;s statement was true.  The results are interesting enough that I&#39;m posting them even though they are pure speculation. I&#39;m using a hypothetical family, the Osbornes to be sure that this is taken as speculation only. <BR> <BR>Let&#39;s look at the Osborne killing and assuming that BTK knew Mr. Osborne and vice-versa.  Then let&#39;s assume Sonny Osborne is telling the truth about his father&#39;s fear that someone was trying to harm them. <BR> <BR>If he was fearful enough to hide his children in a closet why didn&#39;t he go to the police for protection, because it&#39;s clear he didn&#39;t.   <BR> <BR>If Mr. Osborne didn&#39;t know of a threat, not calling the police makes perfect sense.  But if he did know, not going to the police becomes incredibly significant.   <BR> <BR>One possibility is that the fallout from telling police was as bad or worse than dealing with the threat privately.  If he goes to the police and says someone is trying to kill me they’ll ask me Who and Why.   <BR> <BR>At this point the real guessing begins. But suppose: <BR> <BR>1.	BTK and Mr. Osborne were  selling drugs and BTK was Osborne’s source.  Osborne gets a large quantity “fronted”, but Osborne is ripped off by his purchaser and can’t pay BTK for the drugs.  BTK threatens to kill Osborne unless he pays. <BR>2.	If Osborne convinces police he needs protection, he has confessed to a drug conspiracy and goes to jail. <BR> <BR>[This scenario is only an example, any significant joint criminal enterprise works] <BR> <BR>WHILE THIS IS PURE GUESSING  it does explain some of the curious aspects of the Osborne crime: <BR>1.	Why Osborne made no call to the police for help. <BR>2.	Why Osborne feared for his/his family’s safety <BR>3.	Why the children were killed: they could identify BTK as their parents killer in ways that people who didn’t know BTK couldn’t, such as: “he lived next door to us last year” or ‘he works with my father”  or “ My father called him “John Doe” and said he knew him from the war.  <BR>4.	Why entry was made while Mr. Osborne was taking children to school.  It’s easier to subdue Mrs. Osborne and the two children without Mr. Osborne around, and then BTK has hostages to keep Mr. Osborne compliant when he comes back.  [this also would explains why there was evidence of a fight between Mrs. Osborne and BTK, but not with Mr. Osborne] <BR>5.	It makes some sense of the “Coleman connection”  If Mr. Osborne tells his wife, she tells something to friends at work, or BTK thinks she did.  Kathryn Bright is killed because she &#42;might&#42; have talked to Mrs. Osborne. <BR> <BR>This is purely hypothetical, but if Charlie Otero is correct, we might look in this direction.  <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a>  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3229-->
<!--Post: 3231--><!--Time: 1104644842-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3231"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jujuree<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jujuree<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 01, 2005 - 9:47 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Whoisbtk, I think your speculation makes a lot of sense.  It would explain a lot of things &#40;hiding the family in the closet, the Coleman connections&#41;. <BR> <BR>At any rate, #4 is absolutely how I believe events unfolded once BTK arrived in the home.  I don&#39;t know how any of that would effect the profiles that have been done on BTK or if it even would &#40;said, Jujuree, staying on the topic of the thread&#41;.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3231-->
<!--Post: 3245--><!--Time: 1104668721-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3245"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Skov<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Skov<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 02, 2005 - 4:25 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Whoisbtk <BR> <BR>Very good thinking! <BR>But what makes me doubt that explanation is the VERY perverted MO and siganture BTK had on almost all his victims. To me it looks like BTK killed for pleassure and not for revenge. And the fact that he apparently brought his &#34;tools&#34; to the Ontereo house and not just brought a gun and shoot them all IMO makes it more possible that BTK from the beging was a killer for pleassure. <BR> <BR>BTW I read on AMW that a woman &#40;she is a poster over there&#41; used to be a friend to Josephine, when the Oteros lived at another adress &#40;Aalso in Wichita as I understood it&#41; just before they moved to the adress where they were murdered. She said that a man jused to follow/stalk Joephine and herself on their way from/to school, that this man was in a car and that she couldn´t say for sure if this was the reason why the Onteros moved from the first adress in Wichita. <BR> <BR>If this women is genuin I think that this is a VERY importent leed. She HAS talked to the WPD she says in her posts. <BR> <BR>Skov<!--/Text--></td></tr>
